cartridge "FINAL DEFENSE/GAZETTE/1989"

' FINAL DEFENSE
' A ColecoVision game based on "Final Defense", COMPUTE!'s Gazette,
' December 1989 (Commodore 64 machine language FINAL.ML with the BASIC
' loader FINAL.BOOT, disk 1989-12; no author named on the disk). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the C64 original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/dis.txt, and watched in jsc64, orig/run64.mjs and
' orig/probe64.mjs); the pictures, sounds and music are new.
'
' The game: you are the last defender. Take off from the airfield, fly out
' over the sea and the fields where hundreds of ships and tanks are coming,
' bomb as many as you can while their missiles fly up at you, then fly back
' and land on the airfield and stop on the runway: MISSION COMPLETED.
'
' From the original (orig/dis.txt):
' - the route is the original's script ($309A flags, $30CF lengths in
'   columns): airfield, fields, GET READY, the battle over sea and fields
'   (three rounds), PREPARE TO LAND, the airfield again, and further
'   airfields if you fly past; each run's flags: field (else sea), message
'   (GET READY / PREPARE TO LAND), battle mode, airfield, enemies come,
'   bombing allowed;
' - take-off and landing: the stick left speeds up (+$40/256 a frame),
'   right slows down; up and down only work at speed 80 or more (lift):
'   climb or dive at min(speed - 60, 50) x 8 / 256 pixels a frame, eased
'   by 16/256 a frame when the stick is let go; in the air the speed stays
'   80 or more; top speed 127; touching the ground anywhere but the
'   airfield: SPLASH! or CRASH! and the game is over;
' - the battle (levels 1-9): the speed is held at 36 and the stick moves
'   the jet along a slant into and out of the picture; the button drops a
'   torpedo at sea (fast, flat) or a bomb over the fields (slow, steep),
'   one at a time, which goes off 68 pixels below the jet (70 C64 lines)
'   and destroys a ship (10) or a tank (50) it lands on;
' - an enemy comes every 6-12 columns while the run says so, on the sea or
'   the field it comes in on, at a random depth; one missile at a time: on
'   a frame a random byte is below 16 x level, a random enemy fires if it
'   is near the left edge; the missile's speed grows with the level; a
'   missile that reaches the jet costs a life (5 lives);
' - land on the airfield and stop: MISSION COMPLETED, the next level (up to
'   9) with the score and lives kept; level 0 has no battle (practice).
'
' Liberties: the band scrolls in 4-pixel steps (tiles of two half columns);
' x speeds are 0.8 of the C64's (a 256-pixel screen for its 320); the stick up/down also
' moves it in battle; enemies come in at the left edge of the visible
' screen and fire within its first 60 pixels; the enemy's own kind gives
' its points (the C64 used the terrain at the left edge); the torpedo or
' bomb is picked by the terrain under the jet (the C64: the left edge);
' landing and crashing are judged by the terrain under the jet (the C64
' used flags of the column entering at the left, matched to its map);
' PREPARE TO LAND shows on level 0 too; no engine drone (the speed is
' shown); the start level is chosen with the stick; a button (the C64: the
' stick) starts the next level; high score; after GAME
' OVER the title (the C64 went back to CHOOSE LEVEL).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2). Take-off and landing: left faster, right
' slower, up climb, down dive. Battle: the stick slides the jet (left or
' down: nearer, right or up: farther), a button drops a bomb. On the title
' up/down choose the level, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

